WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.140
For an ideal direct-current segment, power equals voltage times current.

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.560
At one hundred kilowatts, fifty volts corresponds to two thousand amperes.

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.540
At eight hundred volts, the same power corresponds to one hundred twenty-five

4
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.060
amperes.

5
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.300
This comparison ignores losses and describes only the segment at the stated

6
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:17.740
voltage.

7
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.200
The animation holds power constant while changing the illustrated voltage and current.

8
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.020
It then reveals the obligations that don't disappear: insulation and connection design,

9
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.360
protection, conversion, cooling and service arrangements.

10
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.860
Don't convert the current ratio into a universal cable-size or efficiency claim.

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.400
Actual assemblies have additional requirements and operating conditions.

12
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.080
Most importantly, a higher-voltage supply feeding conversion near the rack doesn't reduce

13
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.280
current in an unchanged fifty-volt segment after that conversion.

14
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.880
Mark the boundary before using the equation.

15
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.080
The comparison is useful because it identifies where current burden can change.

16
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.560
It isn't a complete architecture decision or permission to treat higher-voltage equipment

17
00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.960
as equivalent to the existing low-voltage system.

